fund-js/13-async-await-patterns/01-map-w.solution:
export async function mapLimit(items, limit, fn) {
  const results = new Array(items.length);
  let next = 0;
  let failed = false;
  const worker = async () => {
    while (!failed && next < items.length) {
      const i = next++;
      try {
        results[i] = await fn(items[i], i);
      } catch (err) {
        failed = true;
        throw err;
      }
    }
  };
  const workers = Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker);
  await Promise.all(workers);
  return results;
}

fund-js/13-async-await-patterns/01-map-w.explanation:
Start `limit` workers that share one "next index" counter; each writes its result back by index:

```js
const worker = async () => {
  while (!failed && next < items.length) {
    const i = next++;
    try { results[i] = await fn(items[i], i); }
    catch (err) { failed = true; throw err; }
  }
};
await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker));
```

`next++` is safe without locks: JavaScript runs one job at a time, so no two workers can read the same index. The `failed` flag stops workers from picking up new items once one call failed.
